course: dictionary
lesson: 15361
title: One word, two meanings: sonny
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Sonny» can be a name, like a friend called Sonny, or it can be a word an older person uses to speak to a boy. Context tells you which. Ask yourself: is someone being spoken to, or is «Sonny» the name of a person?
explain: When an older person says «Listen, sonny», they are not using a name. The clues are the words around it and the tone: «Listen» and a bit of advice show the speaker is telling the boy something, as if they know best.
explain: Check how the speaker sounds. «"Mind the step, sonny," said the kind old farmer» is warm. «"Now look here, sonny!" snapped the guard» sounds bossy. The word is the same, so the feeling comes from the context.
fact: «Sonny» can be a name, a nickname or a first name.
fact: As a way of speaking, «sonny» is informal and old-fashioned, and you often hear it in British stories and films.
fact: An older person may say «sonny» to a boy or young man, either kindly or to sound in charge.
fact: Said to a grown man, «sonny» can sound patronising, as if he is being treated like a child.
fact: «Sonny Jim» is a jokey British way of saying «sonny».
exercise: «"Listen, sonny, you need to tidy your room," said Grandad.» What does sonny mean here? | A loud noise | *A way an older person speaks to a boy | A type of sandwich | A name for a dog
exercise: «My best friend is called Sonny, and he lives next door.» In this sentence, what is Sonny? | An old-fashioned hat | A way an older person speaks to a boy | A bossy teacher | *A name
exercise: «"Now look here, sonny!" snapped the guard.» Which feeling does the guard's word show? | Sleepy and calm | *Bossy, as if in charge | Happy and excited | Shy and quiet
test: «"Mind the step, sonny," said the kind old farmer.» Which best describes the farmer's tone? | *Kind and warm | Silly and loud | Rude and cold | Frightened
test: Why might a grown man feel upset if someone calls him «sonny»? | It means he is a famous singer | Nothing, it is always a compliment | It means he is very tall | *It can sound patronising, as if he is being treated like a child
exam: «"Off you go, sonny," said the old man, and Sonny ran to the shop.» In the first use, what does sonny mean? | *A way an older person speaks to a boy | A kind of dog | The name of the shop | A loud shout
